  "post": {
    "caption": "Margarine is named after pearls. In 1813, French chemist Michel Chevreul found shiny drops in animal fat. He named it margaric acid, from the Greek word for pearl. The same Greek root gave us the name Margaret. But margaric acid was a mistake, just a mix of other acids. A French emperor offered a prize for a cheap butter substitute. In 1869, a chemist patented one made from beef fat and milk. He called it oleomargarine, after Chevreul's pearly acid. Later, the name was shortened to margarine. A Dutch company bought the process in 1871.",
